  "prediction": "**Tennis and Sports News**\n\nIn recent developments, tennis superstar Serena Williams has announced her return to training after a prolonged absence due to injury and illness. The 13-time Grand Slam champion, who has been ranked No. 10 in the latest rankings, has been practicing in Palm Beach Gardens since Tuesday. Williams had been on blood thinners for blood clots and had undergone an emergency procedure for a pulmonary embolism in February. Although she has not set a specific return date, her agent, Jill Smoller, stated that she will come back to the WTA Tour when she feels strong and healthy.\n\nMeanwhile, world No. 2 Kim Clijsters has suffered a freak foot accident that may keep her out of the French Open. Clijsters, who was at a wedding, sustained a severe ankle injury and is expected to be out for at least four to six weeks.\n\nIn other tennis news, Fort Lauderdale's Ryan Sweeting has won his first ATP title, defeating Kei Nishikori in the U.S. Clay Court Championship in Houston. Sweeting, who was a wild card entry, moved up to No. 71 in the rankings with the victory.\n\nThe U.S. Fed Cup team will also be relying on young players Christina McHale and Melanie Oudin in a world group playoff against Germany this weekend. The 18-year-old McHale and 19-year-old Oudin will be looking to prove themselves against a strong German team.\n\n**Internet News**\n\nIn a separate development, Alexa Internet has been donating its crawl data to the Internet Archive since 1996. The data, which are added to the Wayback Machine after an embargo period, have been flowing in every day, providing a valuable resource for researchers and historians.\n\n**Key Takeaways**\n\n- Serena Williams has returned to training after a prolonged absence due to injury and illness.\n- Kim Clijsters may miss the French Open due to a freak foot accident.\n- Ryan Sweeting has won his first ATP title, defeating Kei Nishikori in the U.S. Clay Court Championship.\n- The U.S. Fed Cup team will rely on young players Christina McHale and Melanie Oudin in a world group playoff against Germany.\n- Alexa Internet has been donating its crawl data to the Internet Archive since 1996.",
